From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] map-agent: a block that blocks nothing is worse than no block

Two independent reasons why Client Association Control with mode 0 did
nothing, both measured on hardware 2026-09-03.

First, assoc_ctrl.c aborted the whole request when every STA in it was
already associated with the target BSSID. That is not an error case - it
is the case: "this client is on you, push it off". The Error Code TLV
that 11.6 asks for is built before the check and goes out with the ACK
regardless, so nothing is lost by continuing. Measured: a client was back
on the same BSS 17 seconds into a 300 second block, while mode 2 against
the same BSSID reached hostapd DENY_ACL and held. That asymmetry is also
why the built-in steering only ever sends TIMED_BLOCK (steer.c:232) -
mode 0 has presumably never worked.

Second, wifi_ubus_restrict_sta() passed the AP's band down to
block_sta. For an affiliated AP that narrows the block to one link:
mtk_mld_block_sta() filters the MLD's links by band, so DENY_ACL is
written into a single hostapd BSS and the client just associates over
another leg of the same MLD. Omit the band for affiliated APs and let
BAND_ANY cover the whole MLD; a plain BSS keeps sending it.

Neither change touches the non-MLO path, and neither makes the agent
block anything it was not already told to block.

Verified: compiles clean for aarch64 (EXIT=0).

Signed-off-by: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
---
--- a/src/assoc_ctrl.c
+++ b/src/assoc_ctrl.c
@@ -843,10 +843,21 @@
 		}
 
 		if (num_err == data->num_sta) {
-			/* All STAs are already associated with the BSSID */
-			agnt_warn(LOG_STEER, "All STAs already associated with given BSSID!\n");
-			ret = -1;
-			goto send_ack;
+			/* Every STA in the request is on this BSS - which is
+			 * exactly the "push them off it" case, not a reason to
+			 * abort. 11.6 asks for an Error Code TLV per STA and
+			 * that is already built above; it rides out with the
+			 * ACK either way.
+			 *
+			 * Returning here left the block unapplied, so a
+			 * controller telling an agent to move a client away
+			 * with mode 0 got an ACK and nothing else. Measured
+			 * 2026-09-03: the client was back on the same BSS 17 s
+			 * into a 300 s block, while mode 2 on the same BSSID
+			 * reached hostapd DENY_ACL. That is also why the
+			 * built-in steering never uses mode 0 (steer.c:232).
+			 */
+			agnt_warn(LOG_STEER, "All STAs already associated with given BSSID - blocking anyway\n");
 		}
 		/* no break - checkpatch ignore */
 	case ASSOC_CTRL_TIMED_BLOCK: /* Timed Block */
--- a/src/wifi_ubus.c
+++ b/src/wifi_ubus.c
@@ -884,7 +884,16 @@
 	blob_buf_init(&bb, 0);
 	blobmsg_add_string(&bb, "client", macstr);
 	blobmsg_add_u32(&bb, "block", block_sta ? 1 : 0);
-	if (bandnum)
+	/* On an affiliated AP the band would narrow the block to that one
+	 * link: mtk_mld_block_sta() filters the MLD's links by it, so
+	 * DENY_ACL lands in a single hostapd BSS and the client simply
+	 * associates over one of the others. Leave it out and let BAND_ANY
+	 * cover every link of the MLD. A plain BSS still sends its band. */
+	if (bandnum
+#if (EASYMESH_VERSION >= 6)
+	    && !ap->is_affiliated_ap
+#endif
+	   )
 		blobmsg_add_u32(&bb, "band", bandnum);
 
 	ret = ubus_invoke(ubus_ctx, id, "block_sta", bb.head, NULL, NULL, 1 * 1000);
